#!/bin/sh
# mux-class_lib: the PANE CLASS vocabulary and its ordering.
#
# TWO MARKERS SHARE IT, and they are still two questions:
#
#     @mux-control    who may TYPE into this pane        (the send gate)
#     @mux-attention  whose attention is OWED            (strip, notifications,
#                                                         next-blocked)
#
# A LIB NOW, where three words open-coded twice were fine, because the
# ORDERING is new and an ordering is a RULE rather than a default: centralise
# a rule, leave a default alone, and do it when the logic appears, which `mux
# agent class` is. Before, two copies of a word list with a clean record;
# after, three copies of something with a direction.
#
# NOT THE GRANTABLE SUBSET. `libexec/mux-agent` tests `agent|hybrid` where it
# decides whether a POLICY may grant a send, which is a different rule in the
# right place: `human` is refused there before the policy file is read, so it
# is not a shorter spelling of this list.

# mux_class_valid CLASS -> 0 when it is one of the three.
mux_class_valid() {
  case ${1:-} in
  human|agent|hybrid) return 0 ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

# mux_class_rank CLASS -> 0 agent, 1 hybrid, 2 human. Higher is CLOSER TO THE
# HUMAN, the single direction the one-way rule turns on for both markers:
#
#   control    human is the most RESTRICTED (refused to every sender)
#   attention  human is the most VISIBLE (it reaches their surfaces)
#
# So "toward the human" is more restricted AND more visible, and a machine may
# always move that way, only removing its own permission and adding the
# human's sight of the pane. The reverse GRANTS, so it needs a human (`mux
# agent class`).
#
# AN UNKNOWN WORD RANKS LOWEST on purpose, so it can never read as a move
# toward the human. Callers validate first; this is the second line of
# defence, in the safe direction rather than loud, since a rank function with
# an error path would have two answers for one question.
mux_class_rank() {
  case ${1:-} in
  human)  echo 2 ;;
  hybrid) echo 1 ;;
  *)      echo 0 ;;
  esac
}
